## E02 Verifier to L2 — QUEUED · Kernel · strategy at lane open · one Codex round

Authority: `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` (level ladder); owner direction 23 Oct ("verify the sources").

**Outcome.** The level on the map is real and dated: a dead origin shows L0, a reachable one L1,
a dataset whose files were hashed and whose licence text was captured shows L2, and every level
shows the date it was established.

**Context today (verified 23 Oct).** `src/osd/verify.py` implements L1 with an injected fetcher,
a single GET with a 1-byte range and a 20 s timeout, and writes `build/verification/<id>.json`
against `schema/verification.schema.json`. L2 checks are declared with outcome `skip`. The
`verify` workflow runs on records PRs and on dispatch and uploads an artifact; the published site
reads the `verification-results` branch (OSD-D5). The SEG wiki and NOPIMS
refuse the VPS with 403 (OSD-D9); five of nine origins answered on 23 Oct.

**The design the lane must map to.**
1. Reachability classes, closed: `ok` (2xx/3xx), `blocked` (403 or a challenge page body: not
   dead, level stays L1 with detail "blocked from this runner"), `dead` (404/410/DNS failure/
   timeout after one retry), `moved` (final URL host differs: pass with a `known_issues` hint).
2. L2 file check: for records with `origin.manifest_url`, fetch the listing, pick files by a
   per-record `files:` allow-list in a new optional record field `verification.sample`, stream
   each with SHA-256, store `files[]`. Full-dataset hashing is never attempted over 2 GB per run.
3. L2 licence check: fetch `licence.url`, store `licence_text_sha256`; a change in the hash
   between runs is a finding (detail "licence text changed since <date>"), not a failure.
4. Results store: the `verification-results` branch (OSD-D5), written by the verify workflow on
   dispatch and read by pages; the lane keeps that contract and adds nothing else.
5. Freshness: `checked_at` older than 30 days is rendered by the site as "stale" (E05 reads it;
   this lane emits it).
6. Rate: one request per origin at a time, a 2 s pause per host, a fixed User-Agent that names
   the repository.

**Acceptance.**
- Classification test: four fake fetchers (200, 403-with-challenge-body, 404, timeout) give
  `L1 pass`, `L1 pass + blocked detail`, `L0`, `L0` respectively.
- Mutation: removing the retry makes the timeout test fail (shown and restored in the PR).
- Checksum test on a 3-file fixture served from a local directory: `files[]` has 3 entries with
  the SHA-256 values computed independently by `sha256sum` in the test.
- Licence-change test: two runs with different fixture texts produce the "changed" detail.
- After a dispatched verify run, the published `records.json` shows the same levels `osd status`
  prints on the results branch.
- The verification record of every real run validates against the schema (existing test).

**Out of scope.** Outlines (E03), mirrors (E07), running the verifier on a schedule (R6: on
demand and on PRs only), a results bucket.

**Stop rules.** A publisher's robots.txt disallows the listing: skip L2 for it with detail, do
not work around. Hashing would exceed 2 GB: cut the sample, say so.

**Ownership and dependencies.** Coding lane; paths `src/osd/verify.py`, `src/osd/build.py`,
`src/osd/cli.py`, `schema/verification.schema.json`, `tests/test_verify.py`,
`.github/workflows/verify.yml`. Depends on E01 for records with `manifest_url`.

**Evidence to inspect.** The test names mapped to the acceptance lines in the two-page strategy;
the PR's verify comment on at least three records reaching L2.

**Dispositions** (Codex review `E02-verifier-to-L2-CODEX-REVIEW.md`, verdict REWORK; owner rulings
OSD-D9 revised and OSD-D11; strategy `E02-verifier-to-L2-STRATEGY.md` is the amended design).

| # | Finding | Disposition |
|---|---|---|
| F1 | Probe reads no bytes (`Range: bytes=0-0`) | Accepted: probe reads the first 16 KB, needed for challenge detection. |
| F2 | Output names are `<id with / as __>.json` | Accepted as a wording fact; no change, the flattening is the existing contract. |
| F3 | Schema validity is not enforced at runtime | Accepted: `verify_all` validates every record before writing (`test_verify_all_never_writes_an_invalid_record`, M17). |
| F4 | Pages imports the results branch; only dispatch on main publishes | Accepted as stated; contract kept, workflow now prints `osd status` into the job summary and PR comment. |
| F5 | 403s and "five of nine" are historical | Accepted: the PR shows a fresh run (SEG wiki blocked, 20 others reachable). |
| F6 | Only one record has `manifest_url` | Accepted: supervisor extended the claim; Norne and Poseidon gain their publisher listings. |
| C1 | Classes not closed or exclusive (401/429/5xx, moved+dead) | Accepted: exhaustive ordered table on the final response; moved only on success; 401/other 4xx/5xx dead (`test_every_other_status_is_dead`). |
| C2 | Blocked should award no L1 | Accepted per OSD-D9 revised: `blocked` is `skip`, no level, `blocked_since` emitted (M13, M14). |
| C3 | Sampling policy leaks into records | Accepted per OSD-D11: `src/osd/verifier/samples.yaml`; record schema untouched (`test_record_schema_has_no_tool_policy`). |
| C4 | Record schema change unclaimed | Moot: no record schema change. Verification schema bumped to `/2`, v1 still validates. |
| C5 | Listing and asset contracts undefined | Accepted: supported listings named (JSON, S3 XML, HTML links, text lines); `files[].url` is the resolvable address for E04/E06. |
| C6 | Sampling can overstate L2 | Accepted: pass needs one fully hashed file; any failed selected file fails; detail states coverage "sample, not the whole dataset" (M16). |
| C7 | 2 GB guard ambiguous | Accepted: run-wide budget on streamed bytes, Content-Length not trusted, partial files never recorded (M6). Streams are not retried. |
| C8 | A hash is not captured text | Accepted: visible text (≤ 100 000 chars) stored as `licence_text`; missing URL skips, failure or challenge fails (M15). |
| A1 | Retry mutation survives | Accepted: two-attempt assertion and timeout→success test (M1). |
| A2 | Challenge mutation survives | Accepted: challenge at HTTP 200 and 503 (M2). |
| A3 | Checksum test too narrow | Accepted: allow-list, empty selection, failed file, budget cut tests (M5, M6, M16). |
| A4 | Licence test permits "always changed" | Accepted: first-run and unchanged-text cases with script churn, previous date asserted (M8). |
| A5 | Publication equality can pass at L0 | Accepted: explicit ids at L2, L1, blocked L0 asserted (M18); live equality on this run's results shown in the PR. |
| A6 | Schema acceptance lacks shapes | Accepted: L2, timeout, blocked and cut records validated; v1 fixture kept. |
| A7 | No guards for robots, pacing, absent licence URL, failed file | Accepted: M7, M9, M15, M16 with injected fetchers and clock. |
| S1 | Stale rendering is E05's | Accepted: E02 emits `stale` and `blocked_since` in `records.json` only. |
| S2 | Real listings are records work | Accepted: done under the supervisor's claim extension, `manifest_url` only. |
| S3 | Record schema intersects E03's `record/2` | Moot after OSD-D11: E02 leaves the record schema at `/1`. |
| S4 | STAC assets E04, retrieval E06, issues E08 | Accepted: E02 supplies evidence fields only. |
## E07 Mirror store and custody — QUEUED · Kernel · strategy at lane open · Codex round done 23 Oct (REWORK, dispositions below; no second round)

Authority: `DECISIONS.md` OSD-D6 (mirror policy); Ophiolite E35 (custody registry of every copy);
OSD-D5 (no bucket before E07; separate Hetzner project); owner action 103 (bucket and keys).

**Outcome.** Datasets whose licence allows redistribution and whose files the catalogue has fully
enumerated and checksummed download from the mirror at full speed with the same checksum, and every
copy we hold has a durable custody document saying what it is, where it came from, under which
licence with which attribution, and how to ask for its removal. Removal leaves a tombstone, and a
removed copy is never re-mirrored by accident.

**Context today (verified 23 Oct, main after PR #9).** No mirror implementation, no custody schema.
50 of 68 records say `redistribution: allowed`. L2 hashes a sample of a listing
(`src/osd/verifier/samples.yaml`), not a whole dataset: Norne's listing names 5 entries and one is
hashed; Poseidon's sample is metadata only. STAC Collection assets carry one origin href each with
`file:checksum` (`src/osd/stac.py`); `osd get` opens that one href (`src/osd/get.py`), and the
manifest reader keeps one href per asset (`src/osd/manifest.py`). F3 is share-alike, behind
registration, without a listing: it is not eligible and not a fixture. No bucket exists for this
project; keys and bucket are owner action 103, in a Hetzner project of its own. `build/` is not
tracked and Pages restores only verification results.

**The design the lane must map to.**
1. Eligibility is a pure function over a record and its latest verification, tested with named
   negatives: `redistribution == allowed`; a complete enumeration of the files to mirror (every
   entry of the listing selected by a `mirror:` rule in `src/osd/verifier/samples.yaml`, each with
   bytes and sha256 from a verification run: `coverage: complete` for that inventory, never a
   whole-dataset claim beyond it); declared size in decimal GB under 20 GB and the summed inventory
   bytes under the same cap; origin reachable at L2. Missing size, missing checksum, blocked origin,
   restricted, forbidden or unknown rights each fail on their own.
2. Storage transport is injected: `LocalDirectory` (slice 1, tests and dry runs) and `S3`
   (slice 2, Hetzner, keys from repository secrets, only in the dispatched `verify` workflow on
   `main`, never on pull-request runs). Layout `mirror/<record-id>/<sha256>/<filename>`; beside it
   `custody/<sha256>.json` (the custody document) and `custody/<sha256>.removed.json` (tombstone).
   Objects are public-read without listing; the public index is regenerated from the custody
   documents at build time, never hand-written.
3. Publication ordering, idempotent: stream origin to a staging key, verify bytes and sha256,
   write the custody document, then move to the public key; a rerun recognises every state and
   finishes or repairs it; an interrupted upload never leaves a public href.
4. Custody document, `schema/custody.schema.json`: record id, origin href, sha256, bytes,
   copied_at, verifier run id, the record's full licence object plus the captured licence text
   sha256 (E02), the attribution lines as `osd get` writes them, takedown contact (repository
   issue template `takedown.yml`), and the removal state.
5. STAC: one logical asset with an alternate location (STAC Alternate Assets extension, `alternate:
   {mirror: {href}}`), one `file:checksum`; manifest/2 carries `alternates` per asset (manifest/1
   still reads); `osd get` tries the mirror first when present, falls back to origin on any failure,
   and reports which location served each file.
6. Takedown is a request: the issue template opens it; a maintainer (the owner, named in
   CONTRIBUTING) approves by a labelled comment; `osd mirror remove <sha256>` then deletes every
   object of that copy, writes the tombstone, and the next build drops the alternate; `osd mirror`
   refuses to mirror a digest that has a tombstone.
7. Cost line in the PR: bytes mirrored, monthly storage and egress estimate at Hetzner prices.

**Slices.** Slice 1 (this lane): everything above on the `LocalDirectory` transport, the schema,
the STAC and manifest/get integration, dry-run upload transcript, custody and tombstone tests,
eligibility over the real records (count of eligible records and their inventory bytes in the PR).
Slice 2 (after owner action 103): the `S3` transport, the workflow step with the secrets, the first
real mirror of the eligible records, the public index on the site.

**Acceptance (slice 1).**
- Eligibility: Norne (allowed, listing of 5, complete rule) eligible when its verification carries
  bytes and sha256 for all five; Volve (unknown) not; a 25 GB fixture not; an `allowed` record whose
  listing has one unhashed entry not; a blocked origin not; each its own test. The unchanged suite
  fails under a mutation that raises the cap to 30 GB and under one that drops the checksum
  requirement.
- Mirror on the local transport of a 3-file fixture: exact asset set in the custody documents,
  bytes on disk equal the origin bytes, one origin request per file, a corrupted stream fails before
  any public key exists; rerun after an interrupted staging finishes without a second origin fetch
  of completed files.
- `osd get` on a manifest/2 with alternates: every file served from the mirror, reported as such;
  with the mirror unreachable, every file served from origin; a mirror file with wrong bytes is a
  `mismatch` from the mirror and then served from origin, never written as verified from bad bytes.
- Custody schema: negative fixtures for missing origin, licence, contact, malformed hash,
  inconsistent size and an invalid removal state, each rejected.
- Takedown: `osd mirror remove` on the fixture deletes the objects (asserted absent), writes the
  tombstone, the rebuilt catalogue has no alternate, `osd mirror` refuses to re-mirror the digest.
- manifest/1 fixtures still read.

**Out of scope.** Seismic chunking (E10); `redistribution: unknown` even if the licence looks
permissive; portal authentication; the real bucket (slice 2).

**Stop rules.** None in slice 1; slice 2 waits for owner action 103.

**Ownership and dependencies.** Coding lane; paths `src/osd/mirror.py`, `src/osd/custody.py`,
`schema/custody.schema.json`, `schema/manifest.schema.json` (manifest/2, announced),
`schema/stac-osd.json` and `schema/stac/**` (alternate assets schema bundled), `src/osd/stac.py`
(alternate hrefs), `src/osd/build.py` (public index), `src/osd/manifest.py`, `src/osd/get.py`
(mirror first, fallback), `src/osd/cli.py` (`mirror` subcommands), `src/osd/verifier/samples.yaml`
(`mirror:` rules), `.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/takedown.yml`, `CONTRIBUTING.md` (takedown section),
`tests/test_mirror.py`, `tests/fixtures/mirror/**`. Slice 2 adds `.github/workflows/verify.yml`.
Depends on E06 slice 1 (merged), OSD-D6; slice 2 on owner action 103.

**Evidence to inspect.** The eligibility count over real records; the local-transport transcript;
the custody document for Norne; the `osd get` transcripts from mirror and from origin.

**Dispositions of the Codex round (23 Oct).** Accepted: counts corrected (50 of 68), F3 removed as
input and fixture, external bucket facts stated as owner action not as verified state, sample versus
inventory (design 1, coverage), decimal GB and summed inventory bytes with the cap, one logical asset
with alternate location and one checksum (5), durable custody beside the objects keyed by digest
with tombstones (2, 4), publication ordering and idempotent reruns (3), takedown as a request with a
named approver and no re-mirroring (6), full licence object and attribution in custody (4),
execution in the dispatched verify workflow with injected transports (2), negative acceptance cases
and mutations against the unchanged suite, ownership extended across the E04/E06 interfaces, bucket
provisioning placed here as owner action 103. Refuted: none.
## E08 Curation that runs itself — QUEUED · Surface · no strategy

Authority: `CONTRIBUTING.md`; analogues named 23 Oct (Registry of Open Data on AWS, conda-forge).

**Outcome.** An outside contributor's record merges the same day with one human reading the
licence fields; a dead origin becomes an issue with the date it died; a visitor sees when each
record was last verified and by what.

**Context today (verified 23 Oct).** `CONTRIBUTING.md`, a PR template and a dataset issue
template exist. The `verify` workflow comments levels on records PRs. No CODEOWNERS, no dead-link
report, no status page, no outside contribution yet.

**The design the lane must map to.**
1. `CODEOWNERS`: `records/**` to the records maintainers, `schema/**` and `src/**` to the kernel
   maintainers.
2. Dead-link report: a dispatch of `verify` over all records ends with one issue per record
   that fell from L1+ to L0 since the last stored result, titled "origin dead: <id>", with the
   HTTP class and date; blocked (403) never opens an issue (OSD-D9).
3. `site/status.html`: the `osd status` table with `checked_at`, sortable, from `records.json`.
4. A "Suggest a dataset" issue is turned into a record PR by a maintainer with `osd new <url>`,
   which scaffolds the YAML from the template and the page title.
5. First outside contribution: the lane asks two named people from the Software Underground
   community to add one record each and records the elapsed time from PR open to merge.

**Acceptance.**
- Two outside PRs merged, each under 24 h from open to merge (dates in the PR descriptions).
- A fixture run where one record's fetcher returns 404 after a stored L1 opens exactly one issue
  (dry-run output in the test).
- `status.html` lists every record with level and date (screenshot).
- `osd new https://example.org/x` writes a file that fails validation only on the fields a human
  must fill (test asserts the exact problem list).

**Out of scope.** Automated licence detection, scheduled runs (R6: on demand), moderation tooling.

**Stop rules.** No outside contributor answers within a week: record it, merge the rest, leave
the acceptance line open in PROGRAM.md rather than faking a contribution.

**Ownership and dependencies.** Coding lane; paths `.github/**` (announced), `site/status.html`,
`src/osd/new.py`, `src/osd/report.py`, `tests/test_report.py`. Depends on E02 (stored results).

**Evidence to inspect.** The two PR links; the status screenshot.
